import{_ as o,C as s,o as n,c as d,ag as t,E as r}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Tests and CI","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic/tests-and-ci.md","filePath":"agentic/tests-and-ci.md"}'),l={name:"agentic/tests-and-ci.md"};function c(i,e,h,u,f,g){const a=s("ChecksFigure");return n(),d("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="tests-and-ci" tabindex="-1">Tests and CI <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests-and-ci" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests and CI&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A test runs the code and compares what it does with what it must do. An agent writes tests easily, and runs them often, so the questions are which tests run when, how long the agent waits for them, and which result counts. This page describes the test layout of a Julia package, the three tiers of a test run, and the checks between an edit and a merge.</p><h2 id="the-test-layout" tabindex="-1">The test layout <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-test-layout" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The test layout&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each test file of a Julia package belongs to one group, and <code>test/runtests.jl</code> runs the groups that its arguments name:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">group</th><th style="text-align:left;">holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>core</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the fast tests: each file runs in at most 60 s, cold</td><td style="text-align:left;">by default, and in every affected run</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>slow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests that take longer</td><td style="text-align:left;">by default</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>doctests</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>test/quality/doctests.jl</code> only</td><td style="text-align:left;">when named</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>metal</code>, <code>cuda</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests of a device backend</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>metal</code> by default on Apple silicon, where the package has it; otherwise when named</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>broken</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a file that cannot load, with its issue</td><td style="text-align:left;">when named</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Aqua and, where the package has a hot path, JET run as quality tests in <code>test/quality/</code>. The rule <a href="./../components/rules#julia-tests"><code>julia-tests</code></a> states the layout, and <code>test-layout.jl</code> checks it on every commit that touches <code>test/</code>. A fixed layout lets a script, not the agent, decide which tests a change reaches.</p><h2 id="the-three-tiers-of-a-test-run" tabindex="-1">The three tiers of a test run <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-three-tiers-of-a-test-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The three tiers of a test run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An agent checks its work many times during a change. A cold Julia process pays for the start, the package load and the compilation each time, so the harness gives three tiers, cheapest first (<a href="./../components/rules#julia-code"><code>julia-code</code></a>):</p><ol start="0"><li><strong>A warm session.</strong> A <a href="./../dependencies#kaimon">Kaimon</a> session reloads <code>src/</code> before every evaluation, so an edit is checkable in seconds. This is the default while the agent works.</li><li><strong>The affected tests.</strong> <a href="./../components/scripts#run-tests-jl-and-run-tests-test-jl"><code>run-tests.jl &lt;package&gt; affected</code></a> runs the <code>core</code> files that the diff against <code>origin/main</code> can reach, and the quality files, in one fresh process. A changed <code>Project.toml</code> runs the full suite.</li><li><strong>The full suite.</strong> <code>Pkg.test()</code>, before a push. It takes seconds in a small package and up to 100 minutes in CI in the large ones.</li></ol><p>A measurement of time or allocations is the exception: it needs a cold process, never the warm session.</p><p><code>run-tests.jl</code> exits 0 on a pass, 1 on a failure, 2 on bad arguments or a layout outside the convention, and 3 on a failure of the script. Its mode <code>select</code> prints what <code>affected</code> would run, and runs nothing. The log goes to a file, so the agent reads a short verdict and not the whole output.</p><h2 id="waiting-for-a-long-run" tabindex="-1">Waiting for a long run <a class="header-anchor" href="#waiting-for-a-long-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Waiting for a long run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A full suite of a large package runs for many minutes and prints nothing. An agent that waits for it in its own context keeps that context alive at full cost. So the long runs go to <a href="./../components/agents#julia-test-runner"><code>julia-test-runner</code></a>, on a medium model, which returns one verdict per testset and the failing output verbatim. In the builder-critic loop it runs beside each critic, so the suite and the critic finish at about the same time. A builder runs every command under five minutes itself, and leaves the longer runs to its caller.</p><h2 id="from-an-edit-to-a-merge" tabindex="-1">From an edit to a merge <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-an-edit-to-a-merge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From an edit to a merge&quot;">​</a></h2>',14)),r(a),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p><em>The checks between an edit and a merge.</em> An edit meets three groups of checks before you merge it: the pre-commit hook on every commit, the checks of the builder-critic loop, and CI on every push to the pull request.</p><ol><li><strong>The pre-commit hook</strong>, on every commit: the test layout, JuliaFormatter, Unicode NFC, and whether the package loads in a fresh process. <code>fatou lint</code> runs too, as advice only. The hook is the same in every package, and <code>harness githooks --apply</code> installs it (<a href="./../components/githooks#pre-commit-pre-commit-jl-and-pre-commit-test-jl"><code>pre-commit</code></a>).</li><li><strong>The builder-critic loop</strong>: in each round the builder runs <code>run-tests.jl affected</code> and the <a href="./mutants">named mutants</a>, the round-1 critics run the named mutants again, and <code>julia-test-runner</code> runs the full suite beside the critic. Before the pull request, the verifier runs the <a href="./static-checks#what-a-change-adds-to-the-method-table">dispatch delta</a>. For a part with a spec-delta spec, each round also runs the spec&#39;s checks on the head and pushes the branch for CI. <a href="./specs-and-gates#the-steps-of-gate-jl"><code>gate.jl</code></a> runs these checks of a Julia package as one command, when you or an agent call it.</li><li><strong>CI</strong>, on every push to an open pull request, and on a push to <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>: the workflow <code>CI.yml</code> below.</li></ol><p>The shared pre-push hook runs <code>Pkg.test</code> on a push to <code>main</code> or <code>master</code> only; a push of another branch passes at once, and CI tests it once its pull request is open. <strong>Known issue (K133):</strong> the hook tests the working tree, not the commit that it pushes.</p><h2 id="the-ci-of-a-package" tabindex="-1">The CI of a package <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ci-of-a-package" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The CI of a package&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The CI workflows of a package come from the harness, and are byte-identical in every package. <code>harness workflows --apply</code> copies them, and <a href="./../components/githooks#verify-workflows-jl"><code>verify-workflows.jl</code></a> checks that every copy matches its template:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">job or file</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it runs</th><th style="text-align:left;">required</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>test</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests on Julia <code>min</code> and <code>1</code>, on Ubuntu, macOS and Windows; <code>pre</code> and <code>nightly</code> on Ubuntu</td><td style="text-align:left;">yes, except <code>pre</code> and <code>nightly</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>downgrade</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests with each direct dependency at the lower bound of its <code>[compat]</code> entry</td><td style="text-align:left;">no: information only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>doctest</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the doctests of the docstrings and of the manual, where <code>docs/Project.toml</code> exists</td><td style="text-align:left;">yes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>Documenter.yml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the documentation build and its deploy</td><td style="text-align:left;">no</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>TagBot.yml</code>, <code>codecov.yml</code>, <code>dependabot.yml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tag of a registered release, the coverage threshold, the weekly <code>[compat]</code> proposals</td><td style="text-align:left;">no</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The matrix names Julia by the aliases <code>min</code> and <code>1</code>, so the names of the checks are the same in every repository. <a href="./../harness-command#ci-protection"><code>harness ci-protection</code></a> sets a ruleset on the default branch with those names as required checks, and refuses to run while <code>verify-workflows.jl</code> fails. A red required check stops the merge; the <a href="./../components/agents#ci-triage"><code>ci-triage</code></a> agent separates a real failure from a known one.</p><h2 id="the-harness-s-own-tests" tabindex="-1">The harness&#39;s own tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-harness-s-own-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The harness&#39;s own tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The harness is Python and has its own suite. <a href="./../harness-command#test"><code>harness test</code></a> runs the selftest of each module in a process of its own, the hook probe and the verb sweep. Its CI has three workflows:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">workflow</th><th style="text-align:left;">jobs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>test.yml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>python</code>, <code>empty-home</code>, <code>changelog</code>, <code>systemd</code>, <code>dotfiles</code>, <code>lint</code>, <code>leaks</code>, <code>strict</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>julia.yml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>test</code>, the tests of the Julia scripts, and <code>ast-grep</code>, the tests of the syntax rules</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>docs.yml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>docs</code>, the build and the checks of this site</td></tr></tbody></table><p><a href="./../development#ci">Development</a> describes each job.</p><h2 id="planned" tabindex="-1">Planned <a class="header-anchor" href="#planned" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Planned&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A regression set for the critics</strong> (<a href="./../planned#critic-regression-set">work in progress</a>): known defects, planted in branches, to measure whether a change of a critic&#39;s prompt finds more or fewer of them.</li><li><strong>Tests of the dependents</strong> (<a href="./../planned#dependent-tests">planned</a>): a change of a base package runs the tests of the packages that depend on it, before its release.</li><li><strong>Property-based tests</strong> (<a href="./../planned#property-based-tests">planned</a>): generators for the inputs of a numerical method, so that a test covers a class of inputs and not only the cases that its author chose.</li></ul><p><strong>In another language.</strong> The tiers and the order of the checks work in any language. The harness&#39;s Python uses <code>harness test</code> as its gate, and <code>ruff</code> and <code>basedpyright</code> as its static checks; <a href="./loop#the-full-loop-build-part">the builder-critic loop</a> names the agents for each language.</p>',14))])}const y=o(l,[["render",c]]);export{m as __pageData,y as default};
